{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 43, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a significant medieval ruler born around 1140. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and his wife Hodierna of Tripoli daughter of King Baldwin II of Jerusalem. Raymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1152 when he was only 12 years old. His early years were marked by a regency led by his mother Hodierna and his uncle Joscelin II of Edessa.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n**Early Life and Regency**\nDuring his minority Raymond was heavily influenced by his mother Hodierna who served as regent. He was educated at the court of Antioch where he spent much of his early life. Raymond grew up in a politically volatile region with frequent conflicts among Crusader states. His upbringing was designed to prepare him for the challenges of ruling a border county constantly threatened by Muslim powers.\n\nRaymond III came of age in the midst of the Second Crusade which had ended in failure for European forces. The Muslim world was united under leaders like Nur ad-Din and later Saladin who posed a growing threat to the Crusader states. Raymond III married Eschiva of Bures in 1164 to strengthen his position. Their marriage produced a daughter Hodierna who later married Bohemond III of Antioch but no sons ensuring Raymond’s lineage ended with him.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n**Military Campaigns and Alliances**\nRaymond III was known for his military skills and political acumen. He played a crucial role in defending the Crusader states against expanding Muslim forces. In 1167 he participated in a campaign against Muslim forces in the region of Harenc leading to a temporary truce. His strategic alliances were vital in maintaining the stability of Tripoli. He often cooperated with other Crusader leaders like Baldwin IV of Jerusalem and Bohemond III of Antioch.\n\nOne of his most notable achievements was his resistance against the forces of Saladin who had begun consolidating power in the region. Raymond III participated in the Battle of Montgisard in 1177 where Baldwin IV achieved a significant victory over Saladin. Later in 1182 Raymond led a campaign to relieve the Siege of Harenc which was under attack by Saladin’s forces. Despite their efforts the Muslims eventually prevailed marking a turning point in the region.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n**Later Years and Death**\nIn his later years Raymond III continued to engage in diplomacy and military actions to protect his county. He maintained a delicate balance between the Crusader states and the growing Muslim threat. His efforts were aimed at preserving Tripoli’s independence and security. Raymond III died in 1187 either from illness or in battle depending on historical accounts. His death occurred shortly before the fall of Jerusalem to Saladin in October 1187.\n\nRaymond III’s legacy lies in his ability to navigate the complex political and military landscape of the 12th century. He preserved Tripoli’s independence for over three decades despite numerous challenges. His policies and alliances were crucial in maintaining the Crusader presence in the Levant. With no direct heir his county passed to his sister Maria who was married to Raymond IV of Antioch ensuring the continuity of the Tripolitan line through Bohemond III. Raymond III’s reign exemplified the resilience and strategic prowess required to govern in one of the most turbulent periods of medieval history.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.07479121536016464}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.0001674750237725675}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0035772635601460934}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.22211115062236786}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.0003123987407889217}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -0.016026604920625687}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.4773028790950775}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.1037449985742569}, {"token": " 